// Reactivity S2 — dependency tracking is dynamic: an effect subscribes to exactly the signals it reads
// *on its most recent run*, and resubscribes each time. This effect reads `a` while `cond` is true and
// `b` while it is false. So: it prints `a=10` on creation; changing `b` does nothing (not subscribed);
// changing `a` reruns it (`a=11`); flipping `cond` reruns it and switches its subscription to `b`
// (`b=21`); after which changing `a` does nothing and changing `b` reruns it (`b=22`). This is the
// property a naive static-dependency implementation gets wrong.
// expect: stdout "a=10"
// expect: stdout "a=11"
// expect: stdout "b=21"
// expect: stdout "b=22"
// expect: exit 0
use std.reactive.{signal, effect}
a = signal(10)
b = signal(20)
cond = signal(true)
effect(fn() {
    if cond.get() {
        echo "a=${a.get()}"
    } else {
        echo "b=${b.get()}"
    }
})
b.set(21)
a.set(11)
cond.set(false)
a.set(12)
b.set(22)
